# XSH uselocale()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The calling thread's locale is observed through uselocale((locale_t)0) and
# nl_langinfo(CODESET). Objects for another locale come from
# pc_find_locale("UTF-8", 0); those tests are NOTINUSE without one, and
# UNRESOLVED if the POSIX locale reported the same code set. The may-fail EINVAL
# for an invalid object is not provoked (an invalid handle is not a thing a
# test can make portably).

assertion  XSH.uselocale.001
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       In a thread that has not installed a locale, uselocale((locale_t)0)
           returns LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE, repeatedly, and does not change the
           current locale (the langinfo items stay as they were).
test       t/001-initial.c

assertion  XSH.uselocale.002
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       Installing an object returns the previous setting (LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE
           first); the query then returns the object, and still does after a
           second query; installing a second object returns the first;
           installing LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE returns the object last installed, and
           afterwards the query gives LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE again; installing the
           object that is already installed returns it.
test       t/002-install-query.c

assertion  XSH.uselocale.003
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       A thread-local locale changes what the thread sees and not the
           global locale: with the global locale POSIX and an object for
           another locale installed, nl_langinfo(CODESET) is that locale's
           code set while the global names (setlocale(LC_ALL, NULL)) are
           unchanged; after installing LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE the code set is the
           POSIX one.
test       t/003-thread-effect.c

assertion  XSH.uselocale.004
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       After uselocale(LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE) the thread follows the global
           locale: a change made with setlocale() while a POSIX-locale object
           was installed shows in the thread (code set of the other locale)
           only after the object is uninstalled, and a further change of the
           global locale back to "C" shows at once.
test       t/004-global-follows.c

assertion  XSH.uselocale.005
anchor     functions/uselocale.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       Another thread's current locale is not changed by an install in
           the calling thread: a thread started before the install, and
           still without a locale of its own, finds uselocale((locale_t)0)
           equal to LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE and the POSIX code set afterwards.
test       t/005-other-thread.c
